1. Definitions

"Licensor" means Mister.D LLC.

"Enterprise Software" means the files in the ee/ directory of the Thulu
repository, and the parts of any program built from them.

"Core" means every file of the repository outside ee/. The Core is licensed
under the GNU Affero General Public License, version 3 only (../LICENSE).
Nothing in this license limits the rights the AGPL gives you over the Core.

"License Key" means a token signed by Licensor and set in the
OCTOPUS_LICENSE_KEY setting of an installation.

"Order" means the purchase, quote or signed agreement under which Licensor
issued your License Key. If a signed agreement conflicts with this license,
the signed agreement prevails.

"Production Use" means using a feature that the Enterprise Software provides
(today: the Kubernetes runtime, audit log export and Stripe billing) for any
purpose other than those in section 2.

"People" means the individuals who have signed in to an installation at
least once and have access to at least one of its workspaces. Pending
invitations and API keys are not People.

"You" means the individual or organization that uses the Enterprise
Software. If you use it for an organization, you accept this license for it.

2. Development and testing

Without a License Key, you may read, copy, build, run and modify the
Enterprise Software to develop, test, evaluate or demonstrate it, or
something that works with it, on installations that do no real work for you
or anyone else.

3. Builds that include the Enterprise Software

The official Thulu binaries and container images include the Enterprise
Software. Running them, or any build that includes it, without a License Key
needs no license from Licensor: the features the Enterprise Software
provides stay off, and the rest is the Core under the AGPL.

4. Production Use

You may make Production Use of the Enterprise Software only while you hold a
valid License Key issued to you that names the features you use, and only
within the number of People and other terms of your Order. This applies
whatever the number of People: there is no free Production Use.

If a License Key states an expiry date, for 14 days after that date you may
keep making Production Use while you renew. After those 14 days, Production
Use stops until you set a new License Key. Expiry does not affect the Core
or your data.

The license check runs offline and sends nothing to Licensor.

5. Number of People

The number of People in your Order is a term of this license. The software
does not enforce it: it shows a notice to workspace owners and admins when
more People use an installation than the License Key states, and it does
not report anything to Licensor. If more People use your installations than
your Order covers, you will report and pay for the additional People as your
Order provides. If your Order does not say how, you will tell Licensor within
30 days and pay for them at the price in your Order, prorated from when they
were added.

6. Modifications

You own the changes you write to the Enterprise Software ("Modifications"),
to the extent they are your own work. The Enterprise Software they change
remains Licensor's, and a modified copy remains under this license: you may
use and distribute it only as this license allows. Licensor gets no rights
in your Modifications unless you send them to Licensor. If you send Licensor
a Modification, suggestion or bug report, you grant Licensor a perpetual,
worldwide, royalty-free, non-exclusive license to use, modify and distribute
it, with no obligation to you.

7. Distribution

You may distribute copies of the Enterprise Software, modified or not, on
its own or inside a build of Thulu, if you include this license, keep its
copyright and license notices, mark the files you modified, and give the
recipient no rights beyond this license. Each recipient needs its own
License Key for Production Use.

You may not offer Production Use of the Enterprise Software to third parties
as a hosted or managed service without a written agreement with Licensor.

8. No circumvention

Outside the uses in section 2, you may not remove, disable, change or work
around the License Key check; replace the public key the Enterprise Software
verifies against; turn on a feature your License Key does not name; or use a
License Key issued to someone else. This applies whether you do it by
changing the Enterprise Software or files outside ee/. Code copied from the
Enterprise Software into another file or project stays under this license.
This section limits only how you use the Enterprise Software; it does not
restrict what the AGPL lets you do with the Core.

9. Termination

If you breach this license, your rights under it end automatically. If you
stop the breach within 30 days after Licensor notifies you, your rights are
restored. This cure applies once; a later breach ends your rights for good.
When your rights end, you must stop Production Use. Sections 6, 10, 11, 12
and 13 survive.

